  "recent_commits": [
    {
      "sha": "b30d5b9d36786ff778b4bb7a7e1c4b5d26de126e",
      "date": "2026-10-27T09:35:14Z",
      "message": "FSR: the worker thread takes the engine threads' stack, and Windows exports _errno\n\nglibc refused the 256 KiB stack with EINVAL under the test binary's static TLS, so the Linux arena tests\naborted; the Windows C shim did not export _errno, which the FSR reader and trace sink call."
    },
    {
      "sha": "3f144ccf8ea466d9d29043cabc180795ee4a211c",
      "date": "2026-10-27T09:04:56Z",
      "message": "Changelog for the 1.1.7 batch; Metal link advice in its own file\n\nnative/metal.zig stays under 600 lines with the FSR options. The Flash Next recipe points the\nnative binary's --ssd-experts and --ple-on-ssd at the residency recipe instead of saying it refuses them."
    },
    {
      "sha": "fce7b9b29b96a0e8c1ba4a3f0e491fe9ac59111f",
      "date": "2026-10-26T13:09:57Z",
      "message": "flashnext: bound exact SSD read overhead and prioritize actual demand\n\nDeduplicate prompt unions, index pending reads and coalesce adjacent source\nand destination spans. Queue at most nine uncached Darwin AIO requests and\ndrain every writer before publication, failure or synchronous fallback.\nKeep component bytes, slot padding and all GPU arithmetic unchanged.\n\nForecast only into cold slots without spending actual demand's reference\nbits. Cancel unstarted irrelevant predictions when the real union arrives.\nCharge the new metadata and report lifetime storage and ownership counts.\n\nExtend synthetic C0 planning with explicit component geometry, byte ceilings\nand conditional serialized throughput estimates. Historical bandwidth is not\na measurement of the tested device. This continues our implementation of\nproven FSR techniques; existing prior-art credits remain in the design.\n\nHost gates: 306 steps; 3457 passes, 15 skips. Seven profiler tests pass.\nOld production controls reproduce excess reads and forecast pollution.\nDevice token equality, current-drive bandwidth and speed remain unqualified.\n\nRefs #1039"
    },
    {
      "sha": "f2cc5b9a9165f1768ddddffb3c3595d5149bcfa5",
      "date": "2026-10-26T02:16:46Z",
      "message": "flashnext: correct FSR launch memory and patch complete prompt libraries\n\nDeclare the route kernel's two 320-element int arrays as 2560 threadgroup bytes. Audit all fifteen FSR launch records against their actual source arrays, entry reservations and zero dynamic allocation.\n\nBind FSR arguments only to named gather entries. The runtime six-bit library appends dense and attention kernels, so whole-library parameter and kernel counts rejected its real source. Keep those unrelated kernels byte-identical and retain refusal checks for source drift.\n\nExercise the complete embedded libraries, every admitted bits/group recipe at routed widths, both fragment paths and the real prompt compiler through a recording Metal boundary. Host regressions reproduce both original failures. Actual Metal compiler and device reruns remain pending."
    },
    {
      "sha": "0ec8177822cdf9bc33aee334ee147ff4c32c4425",
      "date": "2026-10-26T01:41:14Z",
      "message": "flashnext: serve exact expert and PLE residency through the native Metal owner\n\nWire complete actual routing traces, bounded uncached storage, persistent slot addressing, whole-path admission and next-layer router forecasts into the existing prompt, decode, batch and MTP paths. Drain consumers before slot reuse and order asynchronous command cuts with the native fence. Refuse unsupported backends and paired execution before loading.\n\nAdd host ownership, forwarding, reader-failure and source-preservation checks, plus compile-only device gate bodies and Metal contract registrations. The prepared synthetic and real-checkpoint GPU gates still require device qualification.\n\nImplement published techniques from EdgeMoE, MoE-Infinity, Fiddler and Fate. Slotstream is an outside comparison only."
    }
  ],
